<!-- WHEN / WHERE USED -->
<section class="content-section" id="when-where-used" aria-labelledby="usage-heading">
  <h2 id="usage-heading">Where and When WEST and EST Are Observed</h2>
  
  <article style="margin-bottom: 1.5rem;">
    <h3>Western European Summer Time (WEST) Territory</h3>
    <p>
      WEST (UTC+1) is the daylight saving time offset implemented across Portugal (Lisbon, Porto, Braga, Coimbra), the Autonomous Region of Madeira (Funchal), the Canary Islands of Spain (Tenerife, Gran Canaria), and the Faroe Islands (Denmark). It begins on the last Sunday in March and ends on the last Sunday in October.
    </p>
  </article>

  <article style="margin-bottom: 1.5rem;">
    <h3>Eastern Standard Time (EST) Territory</h3>
    <p>
      EST (UTC-5) is the standard winter time zone covering 23 US states in the Eastern time zone (New York, Florida, Pennsylvania, Ohio, Massachusetts), eastern provinces of Canada (Ontario, Quebec), and parts of the Caribbean (Jamaica, Cayman Islands, Bahamas).
    </p>
  </article>

  <article style="margin-bottom: 1.5rem;">
    <h3>Cross-Continental Economic Links</h3>
    <p>
      Portugal has emerged as a premier European technology hub, drawing extensive US venture capital and remote engineering partnerships. Managing the WEST to EST time conversion accurately is critical for tech startups in Lisbon, financial markets, aviation lines connecting Portela and Newark, and hospitality sectors catering to transatlantic travelers.
    </p>
  </article>
</section>

<!-- COMMON MISTAKES -->
<section class="content-section" id="common-mistakes" aria-labelledby="mistakes-heading">
  <h2 id="mistakes-heading">Common Pitfalls in WEST to EST Conversions</h2>
  
  <ul style="line-height: 1.8; color: var(--muted);">
    <li><strong style="color: var(--text);">Confusing WEST with CEST:</strong> Western European Summer Time is UTC+1 (Lisbon time), whereas Central European Summer Time (CEST, used in Madrid/Paris) is UTC+2. Do not treat Lisbon time as identical to Spain mainland time!</li>
    <li><strong style="color: var(--text);">Ignoring Seasonal Daylight Saving Shifts:</strong> The US switches between EST (UTC-5) and EDT (UTC-4) in March/November, while Europe switches between WET (UTC+0) and WEST (UTC+1). Always check exact calendar dates for 5-hour vs 6-hour gaps.</li>
    <li><strong style="color: var(--text);">Date Rollback Errors:</strong> A time such as 2:00 AM WEST corresponds to 8:00 PM EST on the <em>previous evening</em>. Failing to adjust the calendar date causes scheduling mismatches.</li>
  </ul>
</section>

